[35.159247, "o", "  qwen3.6-35b — 9 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✗ bash — exit 1\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 10 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ `ensurepip` didn't actually restore the module. Let me try a different ap…\r\n  ⚙ bash curl -sS https://bootstrap.pypa.io/get-pip.py -o /tmp/get-pip.py 2>&1 …\r\n    ⚠ bash — refused (network = false)\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 11 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ No network access. Let me try the bundled pip wheel approach instead:\r\n[approval] bash: find /usr/local -name \"pip*.whl\" -o -name \"pip*.tar.gz\" 2>/dev/null; find / -name \"pip\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head -20 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash find /usr/local -name \"pip*.whl\" -o -name \"pip*.tar.gz\" 2>/dev/null; f…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The bundled wheel is there. The issue is that `ensurepip` thinks pip is a…\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && pytho"]
[35.159293, "o", "n3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify it works:\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me do a quick test install to confirm it actually works:\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\npip is fully working again.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong:** The `pip` Python module was corrupted — the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory existed in `site-packages` but the actual `pip` package code"]
[35.159301, "o", " was missing. This made `pip` think it was already installed (so `ensurepip` skipped reinstalling), but the module itself was gone, causing `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** Removed the stale `dist-info` directory and re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which restored the pip package from the bundled wheel. Both `pip3` and `python3 -m pip` are working again.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=16 surface=de1fadd2f308\r\n"]
